7.2 Describe the snapshot with dataspice
Four prepared CSV files under data/metadata/health-wellbeing/ record the title and licence, creator, download link, and variables. n in attributes.csv (line 4) says that each row of the data describes one study and n is the sample size within that study. The teaching snapshot has 29 rows and five variables (Harrer et al., n.d.).

file.edit(
  file.path("data", "metadata", "health-wellbeing", "attributes.csv")
)

dataspice combines the CSV files into a Schema.org JSON-LD record (Boettiger et al. 2026). The excerpt shows the title, licence, download link, and variables, so you can see that its meaning and unit survive the export.
source(“pipelines/workshop_support.R”) use_workshop_book_outputs()

source("pipelines/workshop_support.R")
source_dir &lt;- file.path("data", "metadata", "health-wellbeing")

metadata_dir &lt;- file.path(workshop_output_dir(), "metadata")
dir.create(metadata_dir, recursive = TRUE, showWarnings = FALSE)

templates &lt;- file.path(
  source_dir, c("biblio.csv", "creators.csv", "access.csv", "attributes.csv")
)
stopifnot(all(file.copy(templates, metadata_dir, overwrite = TRUE)))
dataspice::write_spice(path = metadata_dir)

record &lt;- jsonlite::read_json(file.path(metadata_dir, "dataspice.json"))
excerpt &lt;- record[c("name", "license", "distribution")]
excerpt$variableMeasured &lt;- Filter(
  function(variable) variable$name == "n", record$variableMeasured
)
jsonlite::toJSON(excerpt, auto_unbox = TRUE, pretty = TRUE)

